Line 35 in `compress_file` builds a shell command from the user-supplied `filename` and runs it with `shell=True`. The file only has to exist, so a name like `a; rm -rf ~` or one containing `$(...)` passes validation. That is OS command injection (CWE-78). The line numbers below assume the code is numbered as pasted.

Counting lines: 1 import os, 2 import subprocess, 3 import sys, 4 from datetime, 5 blank, 6 class, 7 blank, 8 def __init__, 9, 10, 11 blank, 12 def create_output_dir, 13, 14, 15 blank, 16 def get_timestamp, 17 return, 18 blank, 19 def validate_filename, 20, 21, 22, 23, 24 blank, 25 def compress_file, 26 validate, 27 output_name, 28 output_path, 29 cmd, 30 subprocess.call.

So the `cmd` assignment is on line 29 and the `subprocess.call` is on line 30.

The `compression_level` parameter is interpolated into the same command, but `main` converts it with `int()`. Only direct callers of the method could abuse it, so I am not reporting it separately.

